- Twinkle dee and dun
	join Tweedledum left
	Tweedledum: Contrariwise, if it was so, it might be; and if it were so, it would be; but as it isn’t, it ain’t. That’s logic.
	leave Tweedledum
	join Tweedledee left
	Tweedledee: (clapping hands) If you think we’re waxworks, you ought to pay, you know.
	join Alice right
	Alice: (confused) I never said you were waxworks!
	leave Tweedledee
	join Tweedledum left
	Tweedledum: The Walrus and the Carpenter were walking close at hand;\
	They wept like anything to see\
	Such quantities of sand…
	Alice: Are you reciting poetry?
	Tweedledee: It’s a very sad story.
	Tweedledum: The Walrus and the Carpenter invite the oysters to take a walk. The oysters follow them…
	Alice: That was very kind of them.
	Tweedledee: (shaking head) Not at all. They ate them all.
	Alice: Alice\: Oh! That was dreadful!
	Tweedledum: Tweedledum\: The Walrus felt sorry…
	Tweedledee: Tweedledee\: But he ate more than the Carpenter.
- Go to the flowers
	join Alice (alice standing) right
	Alice steps into a bright, perfectly arranged garden. The flowers are tall, symmetrical, and facing the same direction. As she moves forward, they begin to whisper.
	join Rose left
	Rose: Who is that wandering creature?
	leave Rose
	join Daisy left
	join  center
	Daisy: She has no petals.
	leave Daisy
	join Lily left
	Lily: And no fragrance. How unfortunate.
	Alice: I’m sorry. I was only looking for the way out.
	leave Lily
	join Rose left
	Rose: Looking? Flowers do not look. We bloom.
	leave Rose
	join Tulip center
	Tulip: We stand still and are admired.
	The flowers begin to hum softly, then sing together.
	Flowers (singing)\:\
	We bloom in rows, we bloom in rhyme,\
	We measure space, we measure time.\
	We face the sun, we never roam,\
	The garden is our perfect home.
	Alice: It’s very beautiful… but don’t you ever wish to see something beyond the garden?
	The humming stops.
	leave Tulip
	join Rose left
	Rose: Beyond?
	leave Rose
	join Daisy left
	Daisy: How improper.
	leave Daisy
	join Lily left
	Lily: Movement suggests instability.
	Flowers (singing louder)\:\
	Rooted deep, we never stray,\
	The earth decides where we will stay.\
	To wander wild is quite absurd,\
	A roaming stem is most unheard.
	leave Lily
	Alice: But I’m not a flower. I don’t have roots.
	join Rose left
	Rose: That is precisely the problem.
	leave Rose
	join Tulip left
	Tulip: No roots. No symmetry.
	leave Tulip
	join Lily left
	Lily: No belonging.
	The wind shifts. The flowers lean toward her.
	leave Lily
	join Daisy left
	Daisy: She bends the air around her.
	leave Daisy
	join Rose left
	Rose: You are disturbing the arrangement.
	leave Rose
	Flowers (chanting in rhythm)\:\
	Out of place.\
	Out of line.\
	Out of pattern.\
	Out of design.
	Alice: I didn’t mean to disturb anything.
	join Lily left
	Lily: Intention does not matter.
	leave Lily
	join Rose left
	Rose: This garden is not for wandering things.
	Flowers (stronger, overlapping voices)\:\
	Back to the path.\
	Back to the stone.\
	Find a world\
	That is your own.
	The air grows heavy. Petals swirl. Alice steps backward.
	Alice: I’ll go.
	Rose: Good.
	leave Rose
	join Daisy left
	Daisy: She was never meant to bloom here.
	Alice exits. The flowers return to stillness, facing the sun as if nothing happened.
- Find the talking caterpillar
	join Alice right
	A Caterpillar sits on a mushroom, watching Alice through a cloud of smoke.
	join Caterpillar left
	Caterpillar: Who are you?
	Alice: I'm not quite sure anymore.
	Caterpillar: Not sure?
	Alice: I've changed so many times today. I don't feel like myself.
	Caterpillar: Then who is "yourself"?
	Alice: I… I don't know.
	Caterpillar: How unfortunate.
	A pause.
	Caterpillar: Recite something.
	Alice: Why?
	Caterpillar: To see if you still can.
	Alice tries, but the words come out wrong.
	Alice: That's not how it goes…
	Caterpillar: Memory uncertain. Identity uncertain. Size uncertain.
	Alice: I am certain I'm me!
	Caterpillar: Are you?
	He gestures to the mushroom.
	Caterpillar: One side makes you taller. The other makes you shorter.
	Alice: Which side is which?
	Alice: The Caterpillar begins to crawl away.
	"mat hatter": Discover it.
	He disappears, leaving Alice alone with the mushroom.
	leave Caterpillar
	leave Alice
	jump cheshire_cat_scene
- Met Cheshire cat
	jump cheshire_cat_scene
label cheshire_cat_scene
[clear time="1.0"]
join Alice right
join "Cheshire Cat" left
"Cheshire Cat": You look lost.
Alice: I am. Which way should I go?
"Cheshire Cat": That depends on where you wish to arrive.
Alice: I'm looking for someone sensible.
The Cat laughs softly.
"Cheshire Cat": You will not find that here. We're all mad.
Alice: All of you?
"Cheshire Cat": Especially him.
Alice: Him?
"Cheshire Cat": The Hatter. He's having tea this very moment.
Alice: Is he dangerous?
"Cheshire Cat": Only to logic.
Alice: Can you take me to him?
The Cat's body fades, leaving only his grin.
"Cheshire Cat": I can show you the way.
His tail flicks toward a crooked path.
"Cheshire Cat": Follow the laughter.
The Cat disappears completely.
leave "Cheshire Cat"
Alice hesitates, then walks toward the distant sound of teacups clattering.
leave Alice
